Why this is a GTK C+Rust native application.

The first implementation (PR #2, now closed) delivered the feature set as a
Node server and a browser UI. That was the wrong form factor: Reason is meant
to be a native desktop application. This commit replaces that direction with
a GTK4 binary built from Rust and C.

The shape follows the pattern already used in acreetion-surf on this machine:
Cargo drives the build, build.rs compiles C translation units via the cc crate,
and Rust owns the application structure. C is used for the graph lane
assignment (pure array work over the commit window) so the layout hot path is
small and testable without GTK; Rust owns the GTK object graph, Git access,
the read-only SQLite session reader, and the AI backend wiring.

Git is accessed by shelling out to the git CLI with fixed argument arrays and
no shell. The previous prototype proved the parsing and the guarded-mutation
contract (preview built by the same path as execution, merge-tree conflict
detection, strict branch/SHA validation); that logic is ported here rather
than reinvented. The opencode session database is opened read-only.

cargo check completes without errors against GTK4 4.24.1. The UI is an initial
shell (repo sidebar, commit graph drawn with Cairo, session feed) rather than
the full GitKraken-parity interface yet; diff rendering, the transcript/AI
panel, and the confirmation dialogs for guarded actions are the next pieces to
wire in on top of these modules.
